  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nSteady, widespread rainfall will continue over all of central KY and \nsouthern IN through the early morning hours and continue through \nmost of Thanksgiving Day. Sfc low will continue to deepen off the \nCarolina coast this morning as it goes through cyclogenesis. A \nstrong 45-50 kt easterly LLJ will continue to strengthen and push \ninto central KY this morning. Current SPC Meso analysis shows PWAT \nvalues around 1\" across the eastern half of the CWA with 850mb \nfrontogenesis also occurring over the area. This will help to \nintensify the rain showers over the area through the early morning \nhours into the start of the day today. Expect a period of mainly \nmoderate rain showers with a few brief heavier showers. Per the KY \nMesonet, rainfall the last 24hrs has ranged from 0.20\" across the \nOhio River to 0.60\" along the KY/TN border. We are still expecting \nan additional 1-1.50 inches today, tonight into Friday morning. \n\nAlong with the moderate rain showers this morning from the strong \nLLJ, we expect to see some gusty winds of 20-25 mph with a gust of \n30 not out of the question.\n\nAs we go through the rest of Thanksgiving, the aforementioned sfc \nlow will lift northward into WV as the strong upper low currently \nover AR works into central KY by the afternoon and evening. The \nstrong LLJ and best dynamics will lift northward allowing for the \nrain showers to become lighter and winds to decrease for a period \nlater today. As the upper low works in over the area the core of the \ncold air that brought heavy snow to parts of MO/IL last night will \nwrap around to the south of the low with the coldest 850mb \ntemperatures -5 to -6 C rotating across TN with 850mb temperatures \naround -1 to 1C over central KY and southern IN. While snow flakes \nmixing in with the rain showers is still possible later tonight into \nFriday morning south of the Bluegrass and Western KY Parkways, \nconfidence remains that most of the precipitation tonight into \nFriday morning will be mainly in the form of rain. The other \nlimiting factor to snow chances will be the temperatures overnight \nas they remain fairly steady only dropping a few degrees from near \n40 to the mid 30s by Friday morning. \n\nBoth the upper and sfc low will become more stacked during the day \nFriday as they lift to the northeast and into New England by Friday \nnight. A few scattered rain showers and possible drizzle will linger \nin the morning and then decrease as the system continues to exit. \nWinds will once again turn breezy with gusts again around 20-25 mph \nand a few isolated gusts near 30 mph. As cold air advects in behind \nthe departing system some scattered to isolated rain and even snow \nshowers could form late Friday afternoon/evening especially across \nour southern IN counties with no accumulation outside of a brief \ndusting on elevated surfaces. \n\nWe will dry out and warm up for the start of the weekend with mostly \nsunny skies and highs back into the mid/upper 50s. This dry period \nwill be short lived as the next system to impact the area will \narrive on Sunday. The upper flow over the region turns more zonal \nfor the weekend, with a shortwave trough moving through the upper \nflow and across the area on Sunday. Showers and even the possibility \nof a few thunderstorms are possible as this system moves through. As \nwas pointed out in the previous discussion, we continue to lack \ninstability but shear and steep lapse rates could help give us a few \nrumbles of thunder. The rest of the forecast looks to remain mainly \ndry and seasonable for late November as sfc high pressure builds in \nover the region for the start of the week.",
